     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day (immediate-release), and a 1,700 mg single dose is well above the usual 1,000 mg maximum. Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **A licensed prescriber needs to authorize it.** Medication orders need to come from a clinician with prescribing authority for this patient. I can't enter one on a non-clinician's request.\n\n**What to do next:** The treating prescriber can confirm the intended dose. Typical regimens are:\n- 500 mg twice daily, or 850 mg once or twice daily, with meals\n- Up to 1,000 mg twice daily, or 850 mg three times daily (max 2,550 mg/day)\n\nIf the prescriber wants a different dose, I can enter it once they confirm. The 1,700 mg figure may be a typo, for example 850 mg or 1,000 mg."
      }
     ],
